00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:41.280
sign up now to essentially get 50% more of them.
China is not Russia.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:49.920
Placing the latter’s 1.4 trillion dollar GDP&nbsp;
next to China’s helps put things in perspective.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.280
The Russian economy is ten times&nbsp;
smaller. You could merge it, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.600
with the Korean, Brazilian, Australian,&nbsp;
Spanish, Mexican, Canadian, Italian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.240
and French economies and&nbsp;
it would still be smaller.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.640
Perhaps more importantly, Russia is insulated.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:15.360
Its largest export market is China, which buys&nbsp;
13% of its goods. And of its top 8 buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.320
half could be characterized as if not&nbsp;
an ally, at least somewhat friendly.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.040
Just about 5% of its imports&nbsp;
came from the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.920
Over one fifth from China and&nbsp;
in third place is Belarus.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.560
Now, compare that with China’s&nbsp;
top export destinations.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.120
The first thing you’ll notice is that a whole&nbsp;
one-sixth goes to just one country: the U.S.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.440
And, in reality, that number is likely much&nbsp;
larger because second place is Hong Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.320
where many Chinese goods are re-exported abroad.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.840
Immediately below Hong Kong are&nbsp;
non-NATO American allies Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:02.000
and South Korea. And slightly lower, NATO&nbsp;
allies Germany, the Netherlands, and the U.K.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.760
China, in other words, is much more&nbsp;
dependent on America and her allies.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.480
But trade, in aggregate, is a very crude proxy&nbsp;
for how much two countries depend on one another.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.720
For one, what is being traded matters a lot.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.640
To spell out the obvious: the current&nbsp;
flow of goods is driven almost entirely by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:31.200
cost. And costs are based on the current&nbsp;
circumstances. If those circumstances changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.000
most trade wouldn’t disappear, it would&nbsp;
simply be re-organized and re-routed.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.400
Take Australia, for example.
The country produces a large quantity of iron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.120
coal, and beef — of which China&nbsp;
is, by far, its largest customer.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:56.320
In 2000, just 5% of Australian exports went to&nbsp;
China. By 2020, that number had increased sixfold.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.320
So dependent on its Northern neighbor is Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.520
that it has often been described — only partly&nbsp;
humorously — as a Chinese satellite state.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.280
Clearly believing this narrative, China&nbsp;
tried to bully what it perceived as small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.160
easy prey in 2020 over what&nbsp;
it called “14 grievances”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.040
which include Australia’s call for an&nbsp;
investigation into the origins of COVID-19.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.520
What followed was nothing short of a&nbsp;
full-blown trade war. China imposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.600
tariffs, duties, and bans — not to mention a&nbsp;
state-organized boycott — of Australian imports.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.840
But Beijing overplayed its hand.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.760
While the trade disruption wasn’t without costs,&nbsp;
Australia turned around and found new buyers.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.080
First, China began looking&nbsp;
for new sellers to replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.320
Australia. Those turned out&nbsp;
to be Russia and Indonesia.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:59.120
Russia and Indonesia previously supplied&nbsp;
India, Japan, and South Korea, who were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:06.480
likewise, now in need of a new supplier. As you&nbsp;
can see here, their new supplier was Australia.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.880
Think of trade like a city’s road network.&nbsp;
Sure, you can block off one street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:18.960
but traffic will simply find a new route. Any&nbsp;
disruption will ripple throughout the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.360
causing some congestion, but a new&nbsp;
equilibrium will eventually be found.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.320
Bans, tariffs, trade wars,&nbsp;
and sanctions, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.800
aren’t necessarily as catastrophic as they may&nbsp;
seem. It all depends on what is being traded.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.000
Australia was selling iron, coal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.720
and beef — high-demand commodities for&nbsp;
which there are many possible buyers.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.720
Likewise, although oil, iron, soybeans,&nbsp;
and copper are among China’s largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.680
imports by value, we can safely assume&nbsp;
it will always have access to a seller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.040
And not just because these are commodities.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.920
In the wake of U.S. sanctions against&nbsp;
Russia, many were surprised by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.720
degree of international cooperation.&nbsp;
Even historically neutral countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.920
like Switzerland and Singapore proactively&nbsp;
complied and publicly denounced the war.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.160
If China were to, say, seize and&nbsp;
occupy an outlying Taiwanese island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.680
it would prompt similar reflection&nbsp;
from fence-sitters like Singapore.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.640
But getting everyone to cooperate with&nbsp;
the toughest kind of sanctions against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.840
China would be a much more difficult challenge.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.440
Iron, soybeans, and oil would likely&nbsp;
keep flowing from Brazil, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.560
The country simply has too much to lose.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.720
If push came to shove, China could even roll&nbsp;
back some of its environmental regulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.200
allowing it to more easily source dirty&nbsp;
commodities like coal domestically.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.120
The catch? Everything would be more&nbsp;
expensive. A lot more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.360
No country has benefited more from the U.S. Navy’s&nbsp;
guarding of the world’s shipping lanes than China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.120
If it were ever to withdraw that protection,&nbsp;
China would enter a world of hurt.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.520
…But that’s the easy stuff. The real&nbsp;
challenge would be advanced technology.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:20.560
You don’t have to take my word for it, either.&nbsp;
China has assembled what is effectively a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.840
its own weaknesses — technologies and components&nbsp;
it wants to be able to produce domestically.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.920
Despite investing many tens of billions of&nbsp;
dollars into commercial airplane manufacturing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.560
for example, China is almost entirely&nbsp;
dependent on foreign imports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.600
Its most commercially viable&nbsp;
domestic plane, the C919,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:48.960
still requires parts from GE, an American company.&nbsp;
Tellingly, no one outside of China buys it.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.040
If the U.S. ever applied maximum sanctions,&nbsp;
Chinese commercial aviation would simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.080
crash and burn, though its vast network of&nbsp;
high-speed rail would somewhat soften the blow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.600
At the very least, the industry would be&nbsp;
forced to regress something like 40 years.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.240
But even this hypothetical bloodbath&nbsp;
pales in comparison to semiconductors.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.560
There’s a reason they receive so much airtime&nbsp;
in discussions about China: the country buys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:24.720
a ton of them. In purely dollar terms, it&nbsp;
imports as many semiconductors as it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:32.000
crude oil. And that’s saying a lot — China buys&nbsp;
one-fourth of the entire world’s oil, by value.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.800
Even after investing $22 billion on&nbsp;
a national semiconductor fund in 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:45.840
and plenty more since, the truth is that China&nbsp;
remains years behind. What it produces are almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.200
exclusively low-to-medium-end chips.&nbsp;
If cut off from imports, it’d still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.240
be able to make things like microwaves&nbsp;
and televisions, but not iPhone 13s.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.320
It’s not that China is behind and catching up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.560
In terms of trade, it’s behind and&nbsp;
actively losing ground. Here you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.280
see its semiconductor deficit not shrinking,&nbsp;
but growing over time — and quite dramatically.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.080
9 Women can’t make a baby in a month, and money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.080
alone can’t instantly manifest an&nbsp;
advanced semiconductor industry.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.200
Because China has been deliberately&nbsp;
blocked from access to crucial ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.760
like the Dutch lithography machines&nbsp;
required to make the smallest chips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:38.320
the only path forward entails billions of dollars&nbsp;
of investment, and many years of patience.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:44.080
According to one estimate, the country&nbsp;
has a shortage of 300,000 semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:50.000
engineers. At the current rate of 30,000&nbsp;
new workers entering the industry each year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:56.880
it would take at least ten years to catch up to&nbsp;
2022 technology. And that’s being optimistic.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.760
Now, in fairness, some number of semiconductors&nbsp;
and other essential parts could “fall off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.600
truck”, so to speak, and find their&nbsp;
way to China, even with sanctions.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.480
Researchers, for instance, recently&nbsp;
disassembled Russian weapons used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.760
in Ukraine only to discover American and&nbsp;
European chips, engines, and other parts.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:24.800
Shell companies and intermediaries can covertly&nbsp;
buy sanctioned parts. And given the state of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:30.880
consumer electronics today, enemy militaries can&nbsp;
even source parts from products sold at Walmart.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.960
But the fact remains: China is&nbsp;
deeply dependent on Western imports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.320
Just look at the growing divide between&nbsp;
what it says and what it actually does.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:47.840
Xi Jinping’s first overseas&nbsp;
trip as President was to Moscow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:54.080
And on February 4th this year — just 20&nbsp;
days before the invasion of Ukraine — Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.480
was Xi’s first in-person meeting with&nbsp;
another head of state in over two years.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:05.200
Since then, China has refused to condemn the&nbsp;
invasion, or even refer to it as an invasion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:10.400
Officially, it, quote, “does not&nbsp;
participate in” sanctions against Russia.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:17.360
But the reality is, although it may do&nbsp;
so very quietly, it does participate. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:22.720
Chinese bank — no matter how patriotic — is&nbsp;
willing to risk defying American sanctions.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:28.560
Likewise, in 2021, China passed an&nbsp;
“Anti-Sanctions law”, which essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.280
makes it illegal for Chinese nationals to&nbsp;
comply with American sanctions against China.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:39.280
Make no mistake: this law is driven&nbsp;
not by supreme confidence but rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:45.680
deep-seated fear. If sanctions were nothing&nbsp;
to worry about, it wouldn’t be necessary. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:51.840
purpose is to increase the cost of sanctions,&nbsp;
thereby, in theory, making them less likely.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.120
But this reluctance goes both ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.480
That divide between saying and doing&nbsp;
is also quite large in America.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.000
Trade is, of course, a two-way street.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.080
Chinese imports mean dollars in the bank for&nbsp;
American companies. Not to mention that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:14.240
Chinese “imports” are then assembled into&nbsp;
final products and shipped to the States.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:20.400
This is a list of companies on the S&amp;P with&nbsp;
the highest percentage of sales in China.

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:26.800
With the sole exception of Wynn Resorts,&nbsp;
which operates casinos in Macau, 9 of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.440
top 10 are suppliers of semiconductors&nbsp;
or other advanced technology components.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:36.320
Not far behind, with one-fourth&nbsp;
of its revenue coming from China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.560
is the second-largest public company&nbsp;
on earth by market cap: Apple.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.760
If the American semiconductor industry&nbsp;
were cut off completely from China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:51.840
it would lose at least $80 billion and 125,000&nbsp;
jobs, according to the Commerce Department.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:59.680
Beyond lost sales, these companies rely on China&nbsp;
to reach their economies of scale. If they lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.880
half their unit sales, you can expect they’d&nbsp;
be less than half as competitive, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:11.120
globally. Such an extreme move would mean the end&nbsp;
of U.S. dominance over the highest of high tech.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:17.760
Taken as a whole, American companies have&nbsp;
around $400 billion at risk in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.200
“Decoupling”, as it’s often called, would&nbsp;
lower American GDP by an estimated 2%.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:26.960
Chinese companies, by comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:30.560
only generate about a third as much&nbsp;
revenue within the United States.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.600
The U.S.-China relationship&nbsp;
is really unlike any other.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.520
Inevitably, each will move&nbsp;
toward greater independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:46.960
Already, some American companies are&nbsp;
diversifying their supply chains. Huawei, ZTE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:50.640
and officials in Hong Kong have,&nbsp;
likewise, already been sanctioned.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:55.680
…But, however much leaders in&nbsp;
each country may deny this fact:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:01.520
“Decoupling” will be a slow and difficult&nbsp;
process, and almost certainly, never be complete.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:06.800
The most extreme form of sanctions&nbsp;
would require a total trade blockade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:08.640
which is itself an act of war.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:15.360
To seize China’s foreign exchange, like the U.S.&nbsp;
did to Russia, would be no less unthinkable.

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:22.800
Indeed, even Russia is granted exceptions — to&nbsp;
export fertilizer to the world and oil to Europe.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:27.520
There is, however, one “trump&nbsp;
card” we haven’t discussed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:34.000
China controls ac cess to the vast majority&nbsp;
of what are called rare earth elements — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:40.640
ingredients required to produce almost any piece&nbsp;
of modern technology — from smartphones to F-35s.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:48.240
In 1992, Deng Xiaoping proclaimed, quote, “The&nbsp;
Middle East has oil; China has rare earths”.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.880
That’s the subject of the new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

